<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<graphml xmlns="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns/1.0/graphml.xsd">
  <key id="d11" for="edge" attr.name="type" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d10" for="edge" attr.name="seq" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d9" for="edge" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d8" for="edge" attr.name="flow_id" attr.type="long" />
  <key id="d7" for="node" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d6" for="node" attr.name="service" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d5" for="node" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d4" for="graph" attr.name="graph_usable" attr.type="boolean" />
  <key id="d3" for="graph" attr.name="categories" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d2" for="graph" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d1" for="graph" attr.name="link" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d0" for="graph" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <graph edgedefault="directed">
    <node id="0">
      <data key="d5">Vizio Smart TV</data>
      <data key="d6">UserConsumerTV</data>
      <data key="d7">Millions of connected smart TVs generating logs, performance telemetry and user/application usage data. TVs talk directly to Kinesis to send their data to the cloud. Billions of events per month.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1">
      <data key="d5">Kinesis Data Streams</data>
      <data key="d6">KinesisDataStream</data>
      <data key="d7">Front door to the cloud; everything is piped through Kinesis. Runs an eight-shard pipeline that has not yet needed to be scaled up. Receives data in different formats and columns with no fixed schema.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2">
      <data key="d5">Kinesis Firehose</data>
      <data key="d6">Firehose</data>
      <data key="d7">Takes the data as is from Kinesis Data Streams and delivers it to Redshift, with the raw data stored in S3.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3">
      <data key="d5">Amazon S3</data>
      <data key="d6">S3</data>
      <data key="d7">Long-term data store for the raw data, kept forever for future use because storage is cheap. Not all columns stored here are imported into Redshift. Was used to reload everything into Redshift without data loss when migrating away from the Aurora experiment.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4">
      <data key="d5">Amazon Redshift</data>
      <data key="d6">RedShift</data>
      <data key="d7">Data warehouse holding a subset of the columns of the raw data. Columnar storage with optimized IO for large aggregations over billions of events per month. Replaced an earlier Aurora experiment, where billions of rows did not aggregate well.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5">
      <data key="d5">Amazon QuickSight</data>
      <data key="d6">QuickSight</data>
      <data key="d7">Visualization and reporting tool. Uses the built-in in-memory database SPICE; custom SQL queries download subsets of data from Redshift into SPICE datasets, making it fast to slice and dice the data and create reports.</data>
    </node>
    <edge source="0" target="1" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Smart TVs send logs, performance telemetry and user data directly to Kinesis Data Streams, in different formats with no fixed schema.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1" target="2" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Stream data is piped from Kinesis Data Streams through Kinesis Firehose.</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="2" target="3" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Firehose stores the raw data in S3 as its long-term home, kept forever for future use.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="2" target="4" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Firehose takes the data as is and loads it into Redshift; only a subset of the columns is ingested into the data warehouse.</data>
      <data key="d10">2'</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4" target="5" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Custom SQL queries download subsets of data from Redshift into QuickSight SPICE in-memory datasets for visualization and reports.</data>
      <data key="d10">3</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <data key="d0">Vizio: Smart TV Telemetry Analytics Pipeline with Kinesis, Redshift and QuickSight</data>
    <data key="d1">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BlCXEMp_lqY</data>
    <data key="d2">Vizio smart TVs send logs, performance telemetry and user/application usage data directly to Kinesis Data Streams. Kinesis Firehose delivers the raw data to S3 for long-term retention and loads a subset into Redshift for large-scale aggregation. QuickSight visualizes the data using SPICE datasets populated by custom SQL against Redshift. Scale: millions of connected devices and billions of events per month. Built with Amazon Professional Services: first prototype within a few weeks, in production in the fourth or fifth week, roughly six weeks from zero to users running reports. Aurora was tried first and abandoned because billions of rows did not aggregate well; data was reloaded into Redshift from S3 and the data streams without data loss.</data>
    <data key="d3">Analytics, Streaming Data, Data Warehouse, IoT, Media &amp; Entertainment</data>
    <data key="d4">True</data>
  </graph>
</graphml>
